From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: damnbitimcrit <333091414+damnbitimcrit@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [CPU] Sample guest threads and log where their time goes
 (guest_profile)

Nothing showed which guest functions take a thread's time per frame.
With guest_profile (default off; Windows x64), a sampler thread at
time-critical priority wakes every 0.5 to 1.5 ms (guest_profile_period_us,
randomized so samples don't lock to anything periodic) and samples every
thread the processor knows (Processor::VisitThreadDebugInfos, under the
global lock; handles duplicated, stack base from the TEB, names from
GetThreadDescription):

- A thread that hasn't run since its last capture (QueryThreadCycleTime,
  allowing for what the capture itself cost it) and is in a wait now (the
  system's ThreadLastSystemCall probe, once it has answered; else a
  capture that stopped right after a system call) gets that stack again,
  counted as waiting: in a system call, or stopped by something else.
- Otherwise it is suspended (with its priority boost disabled), its
  context read, its stack from the stack pointer up to the end of its
  committed region (at most 512 KB, never a guard page) copied by the
  kernel (ReadProcessMemory), and it is resumed. Nothing in between
  allocates, takes a lock or can raise an exception: the stopped thread
  may hold the heap, loader or function table locks.
- After resuming, the copy's pointers into the original stack are moved
  into the copy and it is unwound with RtlLookupFunctionEntry and
  RtlVirtualUnwind (translated code through the code cache's growable
  function table), only for code in the code cache or a loaded module,
  keeping only frames that return to just after a call (or the context
  an exception or APC interrupted). Two spots Xenia's unwind information
  doesn't describe are handled: a translated epilog's stack point pop
  (and indirect tail calls) after 'add rsp', and the host-to-guest
  thunk's saved nonvolatile registers.

Every guest_profile_report_s seconds (30) a report thread turns the
window into "Guest profile:" log lines (also when Xenia closes): frame
times between the VdSwap calls (GuestProfiler::MarkFrame) of the thread
that swaps most, with the frames over 8.3 / 16.7 / 33.3 ms and the slow
ones (guest_profile_slow_frame_ms, 0: 1.5 times the median); the threads'
running and waiting shares; and for each thread running at least 3% of
the time, in milliseconds per frame over the frames in the window:

- own time by guest function (the innermost on the stack; register save
  and restore helpers count as their caller), with the part spent in
  emulator code it called and the callers;
- waits by guest function, with the system call;
- call trees (every guest function with what it called);
- emulator functions, stopped in and under them, with their guest
  callers;
- the hottest guest instructions of the top functions (calls into
  emulator code marked);
- what grew in slow frames against the others.

Guest functions and instructions come from the code cache and its
source map (kernel imports by name). Xenia's own functions are named from
the linker map next to the executable (xenia-app now links with
/MAP:xenia_canary.map; a map from another link is ignored), system
libraries by their exports, other executable memory by its allocation.
dbghelp calls share one lock (DbgHelpMutex). The report is built by
platform-independent code covered by xenia-cpu-tests "[guest_profiler]".
